# i18n in TSX files

All user-facing text rendered from a `.tsx` file must come from the i18n
layer in `src/i18n/`. The app ships with `en-US` and `ru` dictionaries in
`src/i18n/messages.ts` and is toggled via `<LanguageSwitcher />`.

## Inside React components — use `useT()`

```tsx
// ❌ BAD: hardcoded English strings
export function MyCard() {
  return (
    <button title="Reset">Send</button>
  )
}

// ✅ GOOD: read from the active dictionary
import { useT } from '@/i18n'

export function MyCard() {
  const t = useT()
  return (
    <button title={t.common.reset}>{t.common.send}</button>
  )
}
```

## Outside components — use `getMessages()`

For helpers that run outside React (toasts, store callbacks, route
guards), read the dictionary freshly each call — do not cache it.

```tsx
import { getMessages } from '@/i18n'

export function toastError(err: unknown) {
  const fallback = getMessages().common.somethingWentWrong
  // …
}
```

## Interpolation and plurals

Values that need interpolation or plural forms are defined as functions
in `Messages`. Call them instead of string-concatenating:

```tsx
// ❌ BAD
<p>{`Say hello to ${name}.`}</p>

// ✅ GOOD
<p>{t.chat.sayHello(name)}</p>
```

When a new string is needed, add a key to **both** `en-US` and `ru` in
`src/i18n/messages.ts` (TypeScript will enforce this via the `Messages`
type).

## Do NOT translate

- Server-provided content: character names, titles, personalities,
  place/location names and descriptions.
- The `location.locale` chip — that's the authored locale from the API,
  independent of UI language.
- Error messages thrown by the API (pass them through to `toastError`).

## Locale-aware formatting

When formatting dates or numbers, pass the active language so output
matches the chosen UI locale:

```tsx
const locale = useGameStore((s) => s.uiLanguage)
d.toLocaleDateString(locale, { weekday: 'short' })
```
